{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2169-08-07 10:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. I'm Dr. Lee, one of the surgical team members. You're here today because you've been having a lot of trouble eating and keeping weight on due to your stomach ulcers, correct?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2169-08-07 10:16:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, that’s right. I’ve lost about 20 pounds in the last month. I feel nauseous all the time and even small meals make me uncomfortable."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2169-08-07 10:17:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. And you’ve been having constant epigastric pain, which you manage with oxycodone?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2169-08-07 10:18:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, it helps a little, but the nausea is the worst part. I can’t even keep my vitamins down."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2169-08-07 10:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Understood. Your recent endoscopy showed persistent ulcers at the gastrojejunal anastomosis, but no bleeding. Because you're not able to eat enough, we’re going to place a tunneled central line today so you can start total parenteral nutrition—IV nutrition."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2169-08-07 10:20:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I was worried about a feeding tube. Is this different?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2169-08-07 10:21:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes. A feeding tube goes into the stomach or intestine. Since you can’t tolerate food there, we’re bypassing the gut entirely. The central line will deliver nutrition directly into your bloodstream."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2169-08-07 10:22:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, that makes sense. Is it safe?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2169-08-07 10:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s a common procedure, especially for patients like you with malnutrition after bariatric surgery. We’ll place it under imaging guidance, and you’ll be sedated. The line will go into your jugular vein and end in your heart."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2169-08-07 10:24:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will it hurt?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2169-08-07 10:25:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll get medication to keep you comfortable. There might be some pressure, but not sharp pain. Afterward, we’ll start you on IV fluids and begin nutrition slowly."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2169-08-07 10:26:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And I’ll be able to go home with this?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2169-08-07 10:27:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, once we make sure you tolerate the nutrition. You’ll go home on a 12-hour nightly cycle. A home care team will help with line care and lab draws."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2169-08-07 10:28:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, I think I’m ready."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2169-08-07 12:10:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The line was placed successfully. It’s a double-lumen catheter in your right internal jugular, tip in the right atrium. No complications. You can start receiving IV nutrition soon."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2169-08-07 12:12:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s a relief. Does it need special care?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2169-08-07 12:13:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes. The dressing must stay clean and dry. We’ll teach you how to flush the line and recognize signs of infection—like redness, swelling, or fever."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2169-08-07 12:15:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What if it gets wet during a shower?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2169-08-07 12:16:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll need to cover it with a waterproof shield. The home care nurse will show you how. Don’t submerge the site."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2169-08-07 14:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your labs came back. White count is a bit low at 3.5, but everything else looks stable. Albumin is 4.1, which is good, but your pre-albumin was low at 21, confirming malnutrition."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2169-08-07 14:32:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will the IV nutrition fix that?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2169-08-07 14:33:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes. We’ve started peripheral nutrition today and will transition to full TPN tomorrow. We’ll increase it gradually over the next few days."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2169-08-07 14:35:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my ulcer medicine? I’ve been on pantoprazole and sucralfate."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2169-08-07 14:36:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll stay on both. We’re giving pantoprazole IV twice daily to help the ulcer heal. You’ll continue that at home by self-infusion for two months."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2169-08-07 14:37:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ll have to give myself IV meds at home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2169-08-07 14:38:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, but don’t worry. The home care team will train you. It’s a simple push injection every 12 hours."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2169-08-08 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. We’ve started your total parenteral nutrition. How are you feeling?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2169-08-08 09:01:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "The nausea is still there, but I haven’t vomited. I tried a little broth, but it didn’t stay down."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2169-08-08 09:02:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s expected. We’ll keep you on NPO for now and advance your TPN. A nutritionist will come to talk about what you might tolerate in small amounts."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2169-08-08 10:30:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "The nutritionist said I could try a stage 1 diet later. Is that safe with the ulcers?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2169-08-08 10:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll start very slowly—clear liquids. If you tolerate that, we’ll advance step by step. But your main nutrition will come from the IV for now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2169-08-08 15:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your glucose was a bit high yesterday at 163, but it’s down to 112 today. We’re adjusting the dextrose in your TPN to keep it stable."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2169-08-08 15:01:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I had diabetes before my bypass, but it went away. Should I be worried?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2169-08-08 15:03:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not at this point. The high glucose is likely from the TPN. We’re monitoring it closely and will taper as needed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2169-08-09 09:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your pre-albumin improved from 21 to 22. It’s a small change, but it shows your nutrition is starting to improve."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2169-08-09 09:16:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s good to hear. Can I try more food?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2169-08-09 09:18:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll try a stage 3 diet today—soft, low-fat, low-acid foods. Small portions. Let us know if you feel worse."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2169-08-09 14:00:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I ate a little mashed potatoes and applesauce. No vomiting. Still nauseous, but less than before."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2169-08-09 14:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s progress. We’ll keep advancing the diet. Your TPN is now at goal and cycling over 16 hours. You’re tolerating it well."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2169-08-10 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We consulted the Chronic Pain team. They recommend increasing your oxycodone frequency and switching from Roxicet to plain oxycodone solution for better control."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2169-08-10 10:01:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Roxicet wasn’t helping much. I’d like to try something else."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2169-08-10 10:03:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ve updated your meds. You’ll get oxycodone every 3 hours as needed, and acetaminophen for breakthrough pain. Your primary care doctor has been notified and will follow up."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2169-08-10 10:04:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will I need to see a pain clinic?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2169-08-10 10:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, they’d like you to follow up at the Chronic Pain Clinic. Your PCP will send a referral. Please call to schedule it when you get home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2169-08-11 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ve done well. You’re tolerating a stage 5 diet, your vitals are stable, and your line site looks clean. We’re discharging you today on a 12-hour TPN cycle at night."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2169-08-11 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What about the ultrasound they did yesterday?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2169-08-11 08:03:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It showed no gallstones or liver issues. Your right upper quadrant pain isn’t from your gallbladder. We’ll keep focusing on the ulcer and nutrition."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2169-08-11 08:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And if I have a problem with the line at home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2169-08-11 08:06:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Call the home care nurse for redness, swelling, or drainage. Go to the ER if the line is damaged, pulled out, or if you develop a fever over 101.5°F."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2169-08-11 08:08:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I think I’m ready. Thank you for helping me get stronger."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 51,
      "time": "2169-08-11 08:09:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. Keep taking your sucralfate four times a day, crush the tablets. Continue the IV pantoprazole, and follow up with your surgeon and the pain clinic. Rest and stay hydrated."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 52,
      "time": "2169-08-11 08:10:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ll call the pain clinic as soon as I get home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 53,
      "time": "2169-08-11 08:11:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Perfect. The home team will visit you tomorrow to check the line and review everything again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 54,
      "time": "2169-08-11 08:13:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I feel a lot more confident now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 55,
      "time": "2169-08-11 08:14:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s great to hear. We’ll see you in follow-up. Take care."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2169-08-07 10:07:00",
    "admission_end": "2169-08-11 18:25:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "The patient was admitted due to inability to tolerate oral intake and significant weight loss from chronic gastrojejunal ulcers following Roux-en-Y gastric bypass. She underwent placement of a tunneled central line for initiation of total parenteral nutrition. IV pantoprazole was continued to promote ulcer healing, and her nutrition regimen was gradually advanced. Pre-albumin improved slightly, indicating early nutritional recovery. A Chronic Pain consult led to optimization of her pain regimen with increased oxycodone frequency and discontinuation of Roxicet. She tolerated a stage 5 diet by discharge, was stable vitally, and was discharged home on nocturnal TPN with home nursing support and follow-up plans.",
    "problems": [
      "Gastrojejunal ulcer, unspecified as acute or chronic, without mention of hemorrhage or perforation, without mention of obstruction",
      "Malnutrition of moderate degree",
      "Body Mass Index between 19-24, adult",
      "Bariatric surgery status"
    ]
  }
}
